# Host permissions # {#host-permissions}

A <dfn>host permission</dfn> is a [=match pattern=] listed in
[`host_permissions`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-host_permissions) (or, for a manifest with
[`manifest_version`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-manifest_version) less than `3`, a [=match pattern=] listed in
[`permissions`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-permissions)), or granted at runtime as described below. Together
the extension's granted host permissions form its <dfn>granted host
permissions</dfn>.

An extension has <dfn>access to a URL</dfn> |url| if all of the following
are true:

1. |url| is not restricted per [[#restricted-urls]].
1. |url| is matched by a [=match pattern=] in the extension's [=granted
    host permissions=], or by a [=temporary host permission=] granted
    through [[#user-gestures-and-activetab]].

[=Access to a URL=] is the single gate used throughout this specification
and by the `browser` APIs for:

* Injecting a [=content script=] into a document (see
    [Inject a content script](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#inject-a-content-script)).
* Observing requests to or from a URL, for example with `webRequest`.
* Reading or modifying cookies for a URL, for example with `cookies`.
* Exposing a tab's `url`, `title`, and `favIconUrl` to the extension,
    unless the extension also has the `tabs` permission.

Issue: The precise list of gated capabilities differs slightly between
implementations at the edges (for example, which declarativeNetRequest
features require host access). The core four above (content scripts,
webRequest, cookies, tabs visibility) are confirmed identical in
Chromium, Gecko, and WebKit source.

## Restricted URLs ## {#restricted-urls}

An extension never has [=access to a URL=] whose [=scheme=] is not one of
the schemes valid for [=match patterns=], regardless of any granted host
permission, including `<all_urls>`.

Implementations additionally restrict specific hosts and schemes beyond
what [=match pattern=] parsing alone would allow, independently of
whether a granted [=match pattern=] would otherwise match:

* Pages served from the extension's own store or gallery, if the
    implementation has one.
* The implementation's own privileged/internal pages (browser UI
    pages), except where an implementation-specific flag or a component
    or policy-installed extension overrides this.
* Domains an implementation-specific allowlist marks as always
    off-limits, for example to protect account-management or
    extension-store login flows.

<div class=issue>
The three engines implement this list very differently and none of them
specify it declaratively. What's known from source:

* Chromium unconditionally refuses to script the Chrome Web Store
    (`chrome.google.com/webstore`, `chromewebstore.google.com`), refuses
    `chrome://` pages unless a command-line flag is set (and even then,
    MV3 extensions get no `chrome://` access at all; MV2 extensions may
    get `chrome://favicon` only), and refuses other extensions'
    `chrome-extension://` pages unless a flag is set.
* Gecko refuses a configurable list of domains
    (`extensions.webextensions.restrictedDomains`, defaulting to AMO and
    Mozilla account domains) and a separately configurable
    "quarantined domains" list that privileged extensions can be
    exempted from.
* WebKit's engine-level source defines no gallery/store restriction list
    at all; Safari Web Extensions are distributed through the App Store
    review process rather than a web-reachable gallery, so there may be
    nothing analogous to protect at this layer.

The group needs to decide whether this spec normatively lists restricted
hosts/schemes (and if so, whose list), or leaves it
implementation-defined.
</div>

## Granting host permissions ## {#granting-host-permissions}

A [=match pattern=] listed in [`host_permissions`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-host_permissions) (or, pre-MV3, in
[`permissions`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-permissions)) is a <dfn>required host permission</dfn>. A [=match
pattern=] listed in [`optional_host_permissions`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-optional_host_permissions) (or, pre-MV3, in
[`optional_permissions`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-optional_permissions)) is an <dfn>optional host permission</dfn>,
and is not part of the extension's [=granted host permissions=] until
granted as described in this section.

<div class=issue>
Implementations disagree on whether a required host permission is part
of the extension's [=granted host permissions=] as soon as the extension
is installed:

* Chromium and Gecko grant required host permissions automatically at
    install time. Both then let the user subsequently withhold a
    granted host permission from a specific site through browser UI
    (Chromium: "runtime host permissions" / site access controls; Gecko:
    "origin controls"), at which point the extension's effective access
    to that site reverts to a runtime-request flow even though the
    permission is still listed as required in the manifest.
* WebKit's engine performs no automatic grant at all, for either
    required or optional host permissions. The embedding application is
    responsible for presenting required permissions to the user (for
    example at install or update) and recording the result; nothing in
    the engine forces this to happen, or guarantees a required host
    permission is granted by the time the extension runs.

The group should decide whether this specification requires required
host permissions to be granted automatically, requires user consent
before every use, or leaves the choice to the implementation (as today).
</div>

To <dfn>request</dfn> a set of host permissions, an extension calls
[=permissions.request()=] with those [=match patterns=] in `origins`.
This may only be called from a [=privileged extension context=] and only
while handling a [=user gesture=]. The implementation must either add
each requested pattern to the extension's [=granted host permissions=],
or deny the entire request; a partial grant of a single `permissions.request()`
call is not observable by the extension.

<div class="example">
```json
{
  "optional_host_permissions": ["https://*.example.com/*"]
}
```
```js
let granted = await browser.permissions.request({
  origins: ["https://*.example.com/*"]
});
```
</div>

## Cross-origin fetch ## {#cross-origin-fetch}

A request made from a [=privileged extension context=] (a background
page or worker, a popup, an options page, or any other extension-hosted
page) whose target URL is matched by the extension's [=granted host
permissions=] is exempt from [[FETCH#http-cors-protocol]]: the request
proceeds, and the response is made available to the requesting script,
without regard to `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` or any other CORS
response header.

A request made from a [=content script context=] is **not** exempt from
CORS on the basis of the extension's host permissions. Such a request is
subject to exactly the cross-origin rules that would apply to a request
made by the page the content script is running within: same-origin
requests succeed as normal, and cross-origin requests succeed only if the
target server's response grants access under [[FETCH#http-cors-protocol]].
A granted host permission does not change this outcome, even
`<all_urls>`.

<div class=issue>
All three engines converge on the content-script rule above as of their
current MV3 behavior, but they arrived there differently, and one
engine's manifest-version handling is unconfirmed:

* Chromium removed cross-origin content script fetches as a platform
    change (Chrome 73 CORB, Chrome 85 CORS enforcement, allowlist fully
    removed in Chrome 87), independent of manifest version; MV2 and MV3
    extensions in current Chromium both get page-origin-only fetch from
    content scripts.
* Gecko gates this explicitly on manifest version: an MV2 content
    script's `fetch`/`XMLHttpRequest`/`WebSocket` run with a principal
    that includes the extension's own principal (bypassing CORS via host
    permissions, per Gecko's own source comments), while an MV3 content
    script gets no such override and inherits the page's own `fetch`/XHR.
* WebKit wires its CORS bypass configuration only into extension-page
    web views, never into the content-script world; no MV2-specific
    content-script fetch override is present. Whether WebKit ever offered
    content scripts a cross-origin fetch bypass, for either manifest
    version, is unconfirmed.

The group should confirm WebKit's historical (MV2) behavior before this
specification states the MV2 case normatively, and decide whether this
specification describes MV2 content-script fetch behavior at all, given
it is a legacy, converged-away-from state in the other two engines.
</div>

<div class="example">
An extension with `"host_permissions": ["https://api.example.com/*"]` can
call `fetch("https://api.example.com/data")` from its background service
worker regardless of `api.example.com`'s CORS headers. The same call made
from a content script injected into `https://news.example/` succeeds only
if `https://api.example.com/data`'s response includes an
`Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header permitting `https://news.example`.
</div>

