## User gestures and activeTab ## {#user-gestures-and-activetab}

A <dfn>user gesture</dfn> is browser-mediated user input that an
implementation treats as deliberate interaction with a specific
extension, such as clicking the extension's toolbar action, invoking one
of its keyboard [`commands`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-commands), choosing one of its context menu
items, or (implementation-defined) selecting it from the address bar.

If an extension declares the `activeTab` permission, then each
time a [=user gesture=] for that extension occurs while a tab is active,
the extension is granted a <dfn>temporary host permission</dfn> for that
tab: for the remainder of the tab's current navigation, the extension has
[=access to a URL=] matching the tab's current URL as if that URL were
covered by a [=granted host permission=], and the extension is granted
the `tabs` permission for that tab (exposing its `url`, `title`,
and `favIconUrl`).

A [=temporary host permission=] does not persist across navigation of the
tab's top-level document to a different origin; a fresh [=user gesture=]
is required after such a navigation. It does not grant access to any
other tab, and it does not add anything to the extension's [=granted host
permissions=] as observed by `permissions.getAll()`.

<div class=issue>
The three engines agree on what `activeTab` grants and on which user
gestures trigger it (toolbar action, keyboard command, context menu
item; the omnibox/address bar in Chromium and Gecko), but disagree on
exactly when the temporary grant is revoked:

* Chromium revokes it when the tab's main frame completes a committed,
    cross-origin navigation (same-origin navigations, and any subframe
    navigation, leave it intact).
* WebKit revokes it when the tab's main frame commits a URL no longer
    matched by the granted pattern, which is close to Chromium's rule in
    practice.
* Gecko does not revoke it on navigation at all: source comments state
    this is a deliberate difference from Chromium, and the grant instead
    tracks the surviving lifetime of the tab's inner window (including
    back/forward-cache revival), being cleared only when the extension's
    action is invoked again for a different tab or the tab/window is
    destroyed.

The group should decide the revocation rule this specification requires;
"cross-origin navigation of the top-level document" (the Chromium/WebKit
behavior) is the more restrictive and privacy-preserving option of the
two, but is not what Gecko ships today.
</div>

<div class="example">
```json
{
  "permissions": ["activeTab"]
}
```
A user clicks the extension's toolbar icon while viewing
`https://example.com/`. The extension is granted access to
`https://example.com/` for that tab until the tab navigates to a
different origin; it still has no access to any other tab, and no access
to `https://example.com/` in a new tab the user opens afterward.
</div>
